flows.booking-compensation.flow:
// The compensation showcase for REQ-AUTO-4.
//
// This flow is not a migration of anything: it is the fixture that exercises the
// abort policy `onFailure=compensate:<story>`, which no legacy flow could express.
// It reuses execution.flow's scenario names and steps so the two read together,
// and adds the one scenario the original never had — "cancel booking" — as the
// compensating story.
//
// The first migration of execution.flow folded this into that file, which cost it
// three of its original scenario names and added a scenario the original did not
// contain. Keeping the showcase here leaves execution.flow a faithful migration
// (P0-F2).

scenario (onFailure=compensate:cancel booking): book a slot
  Click the Book a slot link
  Click the location field
  Type "indra" into the location field
  Click the Indiranagar suggestion
  Click the next button
  Click the Book now button

scenario (onFailure=compensate:cancel booking): pay for the slot
  Click the booking result
  Click the Checkout link
  Type {data.card.number} into the card number field
  Select "08" in the expiry month select
  Select "2027" in the expiry year select
  Type {data.card.cvv} into the CVV field
  Click the pay button

// The compensating story. It runs with the failing flow's scope and then the
// flow stops (LLD §8.3), so a booking created before the failure is not left
// behind.
scenario (idempotent): cancel booking
  Click the Bookings link
  Click the cancel booking button

compose: book and pay
  book a slot
  pay for the slot

test: book and pay

flows.booking-workflow.flow:
// A story with a typed signature, run as a function (T5.2, REQ-BEH-2, REQ-AUTO-5).
//
// This flow has no run block, so `yam run` runs nothing from it: a `story`
// block outside a run block is a function waiting to be called, not a test
// (REQ-LANG-10). It is invoked by name —
//
//   yam workflow run "Book a slot" --input location=Indiranagar
//
// — and by the tool server over MCP (T5.3), which derives the tool's schema from
// the signature below.
//
// `idempotent` because booking the same slot twice on the sample application
// leaves it in the same state: the page is static. A story that made a real
// booking would not carry it, and the environment policy would refuse to run it
// against production without `--allow-side-effects` (REQ-AUTO-7).

story (idempotent=true, tags=workflow): Book a slot
inputs: location: string, date: string = "2026-09-03"
outputs: booking: string, place: string
  Click the Book a slot link
  Type {input.location} into the location field
  Type {input.date} into the slot date field
  Click the next button
  Click the Book now button
  Remember the text of the booking result as booking
  Remember the value of the location field as place

// The same shape without `idempotent`, for the environment policy: booking a
// slot is safe to repeat on a static page, but paying for one is not, and a
// production config must refuse this one without an explicit override.
story (tags=workflow): Pay for a slot
inputs: card: secret
outputs: paid: string
  Click the Book a slot link
  Click the Checkout link
  Type {input.card} into the card number field
  Remember the value of the card number field as paid

flows.guards-and-compensation.flow:
// Guards and compensation, end to end (T5.4, REQ-LANG-14, REQ-AUTO-1, 4).
//
// `booking-compensation.flow` beside this one is the *policy* showcase and is a
// faithful sibling of `execution.flow`. This file is the one that demonstrates
// the two guarantees T5.4 is about, on the smallest thing that can show them:
//
//   * a guard whose predicate is false never performs its step, and the audit
//     log has no surface call for it (REQ-AUTO-1);
//   * a failing story runs its compensating story with the failing story's
//     scope, and the run ends `aborted` with the policy recorded (REQ-AUTO-4).

// ── guards ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
//
// Three guards, one of each subject the IR has: the page, the scope, and the
// step's own element. The two that are false must leave no trace on the
// application at all.

story: I want to see guards decide
  Click the Book a slot link
  Remember the text of the booking status as status
  // True: the page is the booking page.
  Only if the URL contains "/booking", Type "Indiranagar" into the location field
  // False: the status says "Confirmed", so this never runs.
  Only if {status} is "Cancelled", Click the cancel booking button
  // False the other way round: `Unless` runs the step when the predicate fails.
  Unless {status} is "Confirmed", Click the cancel booking button
  // True: the element the step acts on is there.
  Only if the Book now button is visible, Click the Book now button
  The booking result should say "Slot booked."

// ── compensation ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
//
// The booking is made, its reference is remembered, and then a step fails. The
// policy runs "cancel a booking" — which reads `{reference}` out of the failing
// story's scope, because that is the whole point of running it with that scope
// (REQ-AUTO-4) — and the flow stops.

scenario (onFailure=compensate:cancel a booking): I want to book and then fail
  Click the Book a slot link
  Type "Indiranagar" into the location field
  Click the Book now button
  Remember the text of the booking reference as reference
  // There is no such element on the booking page: the step fails with a
  // `locator` class, which is what triggers the policy.
  Click the pay button

scenario (idempotent=true): cancel a booking
  Click the cancel booking button
  // The compensating story can see what the failing one captured. A story that
  // could not would have nothing to cancel.
  The booking reference should say {I want to book and then fail.reference}

test: I want to see guards decide
